# ── Tauri desktop signing ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMPORTANT: the Tauri CLI does NOT read .env files. These two MUST be real
# OS environment variables in the shell that runs `pnpm app:build`
# (PowerShell: $env:TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY="C:/path/to/reponyx.key").
# Tauri accepts either the key FILE PATH or the raw key CONTENT.
# (GitHub Actions reads them from repository Secrets instead.)
# TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY=C:/path/to/your/reponyx.key
# TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD=   (only if you set one at generation)

# ── Auth Broker ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The broker is a separate process: it pairs devices and asks the web
# to mint installation tokens (W4 — the broker holds no GitHub App
# key; the web holds it). The desktop never sees the key.
# Start it with `pnpm broker`, or together with the app: `pnpm dev:all`.
# The broker reads these from a `.env` file in the repo root (copy this
# file to `.env` and uncomment what you need), or from real OS
# environment variables in the shell that runs it — OS variables win
# over the file. `.env` is gitignored; never commit it. The broker
# reads them at startup and exits if any required one is missing.
# REPONYX_WEB_URL=https://reponyx.bond
# REPONYX_BROKER_SETUP_TOKEN=   (at least 16 characters; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32)
# REPONYX_BROKER_BIND=127.0.0.1:8765
# REPONYX_BROKER_DATA_DIR=broker-data
